[Music]  welcome to the program the battle for  control of afghanistan's panshir valley  is showing no sign of easing with  taliban and resistance fighters both  claiming they have the upper hand the  region walled off by mountains north of  kabul is the last to hold out against  taliban rule there are reports of more  fierce fighting between the national  resistance front made up of militias and  former afghan security force members and  taliban forces  both sides claim they've inflicted heavy  losses on each other a top u.s general  has warned the conflict could lead to  civil war  let's get the very latest now from  charles stratford who's in kabul on this  and we do start with that update that we  need on the situation the panshir valley  a very um  fluid situation at the moment between  the two sides  that's right so yes claims and counter  claims  by the two sides we understand there's  been heavy fighting in the last 24 hours  we spoke to people who were inside the  valley yesterday they said um that uh  yeah there'd been a lot of shelling a  lot of panic  um the taliban  desperate to basically try and keep  seemingly people away as well um  the security situation they're seemingly  deteriorating massive concerns for what  is understood to be between 150  to 200 000 people in the valley mainly  ethnic tajiks  um we understand according to an italian  medical organization that they have been  treating casualties inside the valley as  well the taliban say or had said this  morning that they'd taken control of  four of the seven districts  um that is being refuted by uh the  limited information that we're getting  out of the panshir valley the nrf saying  they're putting up stiff resistance  we're getting reports  that they retook a town called anaba  uh sorry a district called anaba which  is on the southern end of the valley um  they said that um they're reporting that  they blew up a bridge to try and halt a  taliban advance  we know that the taliban are certainly  according to them have surrounded the  valley we also know that the nrf have  got a lot of weapons in there when we  were at the entrance or close to the  entrance a couple of days ago speaking  to villagers just outside the valley  they said that um around the time or  just before the taliban took control  of kabul there was a big effort  seemingly by the nrf to get as many  weapons as they could into the valley um  they say that they've been preparing for  this sort of fight for a long time for  years so it's expected it's believed  there are serious amounts of weapons in  there but of course they are hugely  outnumbered  um the taliban are  obviously very strong and as i say they  have surrounded the the the valley it's  important to say though that this isn't  just one valley this is an area with a  with a number of different valleys in it  um and as we know it has been incredibly  difficult um for firstly the soviets in  the 1980s they never took control of it  nor the taliban  and this has massive ramifications not  only for obviously the huge concerns for  the vast civilian population in the area  but also the politics of this country  the taliban have been saying for the  last well more than a week now that uh  they're at the point of  announcing the formation of a new  government a government that they say is  going to be inclusive of all ethnic  minorities well the tajiks represent  about a quarter of the population of  this country so some analysts are saying  that um they say it's inconceivable that  the taliban could come out and announce  a new inclusive government with this  ongoing conflict  with that tajik area the panshire valley  so yeah there are huge questions about  the safety of thousands of people  and the political stability of the  country in general

